{"id": "section-body-04732", "title": ": Chemoprophylaxis options for special risk groups", "chapterId": "chapter-five", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "MALARIA", "Symptomatic Malaria Case", ": Chemoprophylaxis options for special risk groups"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-04732", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": ": Chemoprophylaxis options for special risk groups", "runs": [{"text": ":"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Chemoprophylaxis"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "options"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "special"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "risk"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "groups"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 2, "numberingId": "1748"}}, {"id": "body-04733", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "The term chemoprophylaxis is used to describe the administration of an antimalarial drug or drug combination in such a way that blood levels are maintained above the inhibitory level of survival of the local strains of parasite for the whole of the period at risk and, in the case of travelers, for an appropriate period afterwards in order to kill emerging liver forms. The recommended medicines are atovaquone-proguanil, doxycycline, and mefloquine.", "runs": [{"text": "The term chemoprophylaxis is used to describe the administration of an antimalarial drug or drug combination in such a way that"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "blood"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "levels"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "maintained"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "above"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "inhibitory"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "level"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "survival"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "local"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "strains"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "parasite"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "for"}, {"text": " "}, {"t